course: dictionary
lesson: 7308
title: Pity: feeling sorry, and what a pity
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pity is a feeling of sadness for someone who is suffering or in trouble. When you feel pity, your heart goes out to them. Context tells you which kind of pity a writer means.
explain: «What a pity!» means it is a shame that something happened. «It's a pity the match was cancelled» means the match did not happen, and people wish it had. The words around pity give you the clue, so read them carefully.
explain: To pity someone means to feel sorry for them. In stories, pity can show a kind heart, but it can also mean someone looks down on another person. Ask yourself: does the writer seem kind, or does the character seem to think they are better?
fact: Pity can be a noun or a verb: «She felt pity for the puppy» (noun), and «I pity him» (verb).
fact: «Take pity on» means help someone because you feel sorry for them.
fact: «Out of pity» means because you feel sorry for someone.
fact: «Bummer» (older US) means what a pity.
fact: When a big sibling always wins, the younger one may give up, and letting them win on purpose often feels like pity, not a real win.
exercise: The team said «What a pity!» when the rain stopped the match. What does it mean? | Wow, what a great prize | It means the rain was very loud | *It is a shame that the match was stopped | It means the match was a great success
exercise: «She felt pity for the lost puppy.» What does this sentence show? | She was angry with the puppy | She was bored by the puppy | *She felt sorry for the puppy | She wanted to sell the puppy
exercise: «He pitied the boy who had fallen off his bike.» What does pitied mean here? | *Felt sorry for | Shouted to | Laughed at | Forgot about
test: Letting a younger sibling win on purpose often feels like pity. Why? | Because it is always a loud game | *Because it shows you feel sorry for them, not that they really beat you | Because it makes the game longer | Because it shows you won a fair game
test: Alice says she does not think it is at all a pity. What does she mean? | She feels very sorry for the Queen | She is sure it is a shame | She is hungry and wants lunch | *She thinks the Duchess deserves what happened, so she does not feel sorry
exam: Which phrase means help someone because you feel sorry for them? | *Take pity on | Take a turn at | Keep an eye on | Make a fuss over
